I’ll first inspect the specimen metadata, pattern, and the frozen analysis tooling/reference index. Then I’ll build and verify a complete multiphase GSAS-II refinement, preserving physical checkpoints and only branching on coherent residual peak families.

The chemistry points to the ZnO–WO₃ reaction at 700 °C, so ZnWO₄ is the ordinary product hypothesis, with unreacted ZnO/WO₃ or alternate zinc tungstates as residual candidates. I’m querying only those chemically supported entries first and independently mapping the observed peak families before refinement.

Peak-family mapping now gives a clean two-phase hypothesis: monoclinic wolframite ZnWO₄ accounts for the dominant sequence (15.78, 19.21, 24.11/24.79, 30.76/30.98, 31.49°…), while monoclinic WO₃ accounts coherently for the otherwise unassigned families at 23.41, 26.97, 28.90/29.23, 33.59/34.49, 42.19, 44.56/45.70, and 47.47°. I’m testing the equivalent WO₃ references and a ZnO branch quantitatively; ZnO will only be retained if it has an independent positive scale and residual support.

The three-phase branch remains chemically and crystallographically plausible: ZnO refines to about 4 wt% with a sensible wurtzite cell, but its early Rwp benefit is small because many lines overlap the two dominant phases. I’m now broadening each phase independently (size first, then microstrain); this is the key test for whether the ZnO scale survives once peak-shape mismatches can no longer masquerade as an extra phase.

The coordinate-start audit converged reproducibly for the major wolframite phase (Zn y ≈ 0.690, W y ≈ 0.186), but WO₃ heavy-atom refinements wandered among nearly equal minima and one coordinate hit its bound. I’m rolling those WO₃ shifts back to the published neutron geometry and refining only the well-supported ZnWO₄ coordinates; that preserves the physical model even though the unconstrained branch was numerically a little better.

The [111] March–Dollase branch is decisively supported: it drops Rwp from about 15.2% to 6.84% with a moderate coefficient of 0.713, while all other low-index axes are much worse. With that texture included, bounded oxygen refinement gives a physical ZnO₆/WO₆ geometry and a settled Rwp of 6.60%. I’m proceeding to the final supported profile terms and will then re-test phase inventory and full-grid replay consistency.

The accepted three-phase model is now chemically and geometrically consistent: wolframite ZnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and minor wurtzite ZnO. I’m finishing convergence, then I’ll run leave-one-phase-out checks and a fresh GSAS-II replay before exporting the deliverables.

The phase-profile refinement has settled reproducibly at Rwp 5.649% with fractions 84.32% ZnWO₄, 10.57% WO₃, and 5.11% ZnO. I’m now testing only the common instrumental Gaussian width terms; I’ll retain them only if they remain positive across the measured range and improve the full-pattern fit without destabilizing phase sizes.

The omission checks are decisive: removing ZnO raises Rwp from 5.61% to 6.88% and exposes its 32.05°, 34.93°, 36.60°, 56.81°, and 63.39° family; removing WO₃ raises Rwp to about 14.5%. I’m making one final intensity-model check on WO₃ because its indexed peaks at 29.2° and 34.4° dominate the remaining residual, while keeping the published physical WO₃ coordinates fixed.

WO₃ shows a clear, moderate [100] March–Dollase effect (coefficient 1.118), lowering Rwp to 5.331% while remaining well inside bounds; competing axes are substantially worse. This accounts for relative-intensity mismatch without distorting the WO₃ structure. All remaining coherent peaks belong to the three accepted phases, so I’m stopping phase/prototype search and moving to final replay/export.

The clean final checkpoint converged with maximum shift/esd 0.004. A separate read-only GSAS-II forward replay regenerated all structure factors on all 8,301 points, left the project unchanged, and raised no profile or geometry review flags; it reproduces fractions of 0.84306, 0.10726, and 0.04969.

The deliverables are written and pass the submission parsers: three normalized phase records, all CIFs pass occupancy/contact checks, and `fit.csv` exactly covers the original grid. I’m doing one last audit from the actual final checkpoint so the omission statistics quoted in the report are directly comparable to the submitted model.

The final audit caught an important reproducibility detail: merely querying GSAS-II’s frozen-variable list caused its scripting layer to rewrite the project pickle. I’m restoring the byte-identical, already replay-verified checkpoint and will verify its hash without reopening it.

Completed and replay-verified on all 8,301 observations.

